From 7e8dcccacfc81cb51d578a35ac1207b33117e7b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 13:36:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/21] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon memory objects, VA heap and
 bind/unbind
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Milestone items 3, 4 and 5. The driver can now hold a buffer: allocate
memory, reserve GPU address space and map one into the other.

Memory is NvMap-backed through horizon_gpu_mem. map() has nothing to do
— the storage behind an NvMap object is ordinary process memory that was
registered with the service, so it has a CPU address for its whole
lifetime and every map hands back the same pointer; nvkmd core does the
reference counting. sync_to_gpu and sync_from_gpu are the cache
maintenance homebrew heap memory needs, and horizon_gpu decides whether
it is required from the policy recorded at creation rather than from the
call site. log_handle is the in-process NvMap handle: the closest thing
this platform has to the GEM handle the field was named after, and a
real identifier rather than a number invented to fill it.

Three requests are refused by name instead of quietly downgraded:
NVKMD_MEM_VRAM (GM20B has none), NVKMD_MEM_SHARED (no dma-buf, and
nvkmd_info already says so) and NVKMD_MEM_MAP_FIXED (NvMap chooses the
CPU address; honouring the call at a different one would be worse than
failing).

VA reservation picks the address space half rather than assuming one.
Horizon's GPU address space has a small-page region and a big-page one,
and a reservation lives entirely in one: horizon_gpu_vm_map uses the
reservation's page size for every mapping inside it. The big-page half
is chosen when both the size and the alignment are multiples of the big
page, which is exactly when every mapping inside can satisfy the
alignment the map call will demand.

Three VA flags fail by name for one reason each: ALLOC_FIXED, because
NVGPU_AS_IOCTL_ALLOC_SPACE lets the kernel choose the base and there is
no "place it here" form — answering with a different address is the one
thing a caller asking for a fixed one cannot tolerate; SPARSE, because
that is decision D12 and NVK would otherwise advertise sparseBinding on
this chip; and REPLAY, which needs fixed addresses for the same reason
as the first.

nvkmd unbinds by (offset, range) and never hands back what bind_mem
returned, so the VA remembers what it mapped where. An exact match is
required: horizon_gpu maps and unmaps whole mappings, and unbinding half
of one would leave the address space and that list disagreeing.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 items 3-5
Evidence: libnvk.a is 94 482 bytes with all five backend objects in the
  archive and 0 TLS relocations; scripts/build-mesa-nvk.sh builds it.
